\description{
Implements the algorithm of Allen \emph{et al.} (2013) for supervised
dimension-reduction with optional sparsity (\eqn{\ell_1}) or ridge
(\eqn{\ell_2}) penalties \strong{and} the generalised extension that operates
in a user-supplied quadratic form \eqn{Q}.
}
\details{
Unlike \code{genpls()}, which handles separate row and column metrics (\code{Mx},
\code{Ax}, \code{My}, \code{Ay}) with a Gram–Schmidt orthogonalisation step, \code{rpls()}
uses a single metric \code{Q} and the simpler penalised updates of Allen et al.
}
\section{Method}{

The routine follows Algorithm 1 of Allen \emph{et al.} (2013, \emph{Stat.
Anal. Data Min.}, 6 : 302–314) — see the paper for details. Briefly, with
\eqn{C = X^\top Y} the cross-product of the (preprocessed) blocks, each
component maximises
\deqn{\max_{u,v}\; v^\top Q C u - \lambda \, P(v)}
with \eqn{Q = I_p} for standard RPLS. The alternating updates are:
\eqn{u \leftarrow C^\top Q v / \|C^\top Q v\|_2}, then a penalised
(possibly non-negative) regression for \eqn{v}, normalised in the
\eqn{Q}-norm.
}

